### `aitm config`

Manage aitm configuration (GitLab host, API token, environment variables). Configuration is stored in `~/.aitm/config.json`.

#### `aitm config set-host <host>`

Set the GitLab host URL. Defaults to `https://gitlab.com` if not set.

```bash
aitm config set-host https://source.tui
```

| Argument | Description |
|---|---|
| `<host>` | The GitLab instance URL (e.g. `https://gitlab.com`, `https://source.tui`) |

#### `aitm config set-token <token>`

Set the GitLab API token used to authenticate API requests. The token is stored in `~/.aitm/config.json`.

```bash
aitm config set-token glpat-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
```

| Argument | Description |
|---|---|
| `<token>` | A GitLab personal access token with API read access |

#### `aitm config set-assistants <assistants...>`

Set the AI assistants to install for. Only tools matching the configured assistants will be installed. Omit or pass an empty list to install for all assistants.

```bash
aitm config set-assistants q kiro
```

| Argument | Description |
|---|---|
| `<assistants...>` | One or more assistant names (e.g. `q`, `kiro`). Valid assistants: `q`, `kiro`. |

#### `aitm config set-env <key> <value>`

Set an environment variable that can be used as a placeholder in manifest files. Placeholders use `${KEY}` syntax and are replaced during install.

```bash
aitm config set-env TEAM_NAME backend
aitm config set-env API_TOKEN secret-value --secret
```

| Argument / Option | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `<key>` | Yes | The environment variable name (used as `${KEY}` in manifests) |
| `<value>` | Yes | The value to set |
| `--secret` | No | Mark the value as secret so it is masked with asterisks in `aitm config show` output |

#### `aitm config unset-env <key>`

Remove a previously set environment variable from the configuration.

```bash
aitm config unset-env TEAM_NAME
```

| Argument | Description |
|---|---|
| `<key>` | The environment variable name to remove |

#### Built-in Environment Variables

The following variables are automatically available during `aitm install` without needing `aitm config set-env`:

| Variable | Description |
|---|---|
| `/home/javierfernandez/excellence` | The directory where `aitm install` is run (no trailing slash). In the CLI this is `process.cwd()`; in the Electron app it is the selected project folder. |

Built-in variables always override user-defined values with the same name.

#### `aitm config show`

Display the current aitm configuration including host, token (masked), and environment variables. Secret values are shown as asterisks.

```bash
aitm config show
```

#### `aitm config setup`

Interactive wizard that guides you through initial aitm configuration in a single session. The wizard walks through four steps:

1. **Host** — set the GitLab host URL (defaults to `https://source.tui`)
2. **Token** — set a GitLab personal access token (requires `read_api` scope)
3. **Assistants** — set which AI assistants to install for (e.g. `q kiro`)
4. **Githooks** — optionally install global Git hooks to run `aitm install` after clone/pull

```bash
aitm config setup
```

If a configuration already exists, current values are shown as defaults.

#### `aitm config source add <repo>`

Add a source repository for search and discovery. Source repositories are searched when running `aitm search`. A set of built-in sources are always available.

```bash
aitm config source add group/my-tools
aitm config source add group/my-tools --ref v1.0.0
aitm config source add group/my-tools --path rules/
```

| Option | Description |
|---|---|
| `--ref <ref>` | Pin to a specific Git ref (branch, tag, or commit SHA) |
| `--path <path>` | Scope search to a subdirectory within the repository |

#### `aitm config source remove <repo>`

Remove a user-added source repository. Built-in sources cannot be removed.

```bash
aitm config source remove group/my-tools
```

| Argument | Description |
|---|---|
| `<repo>` | The repository path to remove |

#### `aitm config source list`

List all configured source repositories. Built-in sources are marked with `[built-in]`.

```bash
aitm config source list
```

