### `aitm clean`

Remove all files installed by aitm from the target directories without modifying the manifest or lock file. Cleans the following directories:

- `.amazonq/rules/aitm/`
- `.kiro/skills/aitm-*/`
- `.kiro/steering/aitm/`
- `.kiro/agents/aitm-*` (covers both `agent` and `default-agent`)
- `.kiro/<resource_name>/` (for each `resource` target in the manifest)

```bash
aitm clean
```

---

### `aitm search <query>`

Search for files across all configured source repositories by filename. Results are numbered and cached so they can be referenced with `@N` in `aitm add file` and `aitm show`.

```bash
aitm search kafka
```

Example output:

```
  [1] dx/ai-rules :: rules/kafka-best-practices.md
  [2] dx/ai-rules :: rules/kafka-consumer-patterns.md
  [3] dx/ai-skills :: skills/kafka-debugging.md
```

Search matches file paths case-insensitively. Results come from all sources (built-in and user-added via `aitm config source add`).

Manifest files (`*.aitools.json`) are shown with an `[extend]` badge. In interactive mode, selecting a manifest file offers to extend from it instead of adding it as a tool.

---

### `aitm show <ref>`

Display the content of a file from the last search results. Use `@N` to reference a search result by its number. Markdown files (`.md`) are rendered with formatting in the terminal; other files are displayed as raw text.

```bash
aitm show @1          # show search result #1 (rendered if .md)
aitm show @3          # show search result #3
```

| Argument | Description |
|---|---|
| `<ref>` | A search result reference in `@N` format (e.g. `@1`, `@2`) |

---

### Discovery Workflow

The source registry, search, show, and `@N` shortcuts work together to provide a streamlined discovery-to-add workflow:

```bash
# 1. Add a custom source repository (built-in sources are always available)
aitm config source add group/my-team-tools

# 2. Search for files across all sources
aitm search kafka

# 3. Preview a result
aitm show @1

# 4. Add it to your manifest
aitm add file @1 --target rule
```

Manifest files (`*.aitools.json`) discovered via search or browse are automatically recognized. In interactive mode, selecting one offers to extend from it rather than add it as a tool.

